      <div type="clause" n="4">
        <head><ref type="lawCode" target="law:QuadrDed">Quadr Ded</ref> 4</head>
        <ab type="liebermannText" xml:lang="la" source="#liebermann" n="Quadr Ded 4" corresp="#wit-lat"><pb n="529" ed="#liebermann"/>Opus enim, quod tuis exhortationibus et inconsulta, fateor, pollicitatione suscepi, maius est et creb[r]escentium<ptr type="liebermannNote" n="3" target="#QuadrDed_4.lnote.oe.3"/> cotidie malorum innouatione<ptr type="liebermannNote" n="dd" target="#QuadrDed_4.lnote.oe.dd"/> profusum exaggerationibus, permutationibus, permixturis, ex oppositis, ex adiunctis, rerum circumstanciis, contingentium<ptr type="liebermannNote" n="e" target="#QuadrDed_4.lnote.oe.e"/> modis.</ab>
        <note type="editorial">New row (October 2026): Liebermann’s text transcribed from Gesetze I p. 529 (two independent readings of the page images, differences settled against the page)</note>
        <note type="liebermannNote" subtype="textual" xml:id="QuadrDed_4.lnote.oe.dd" n="dd" source="#liebermann"><bibl><biblScope unit="volume">1</biblScope><biblScope unit="page">529</biblScope></bibl><seg type="original" xml:lang="de"><hi rend="italic">vgl. u.</hi> 34; <hi rend="italic">Hn</hi> 6, 2: noua nocendi fraus.</seg><seg type="translation" xml:lang="en" source="#claude"><hi rend="italic">cf. below</hi> 34; <hi rend="italic">Hn</hi> 6, 2: noua nocendi fraus.</seg></note>
        <note type="liebermannNote" subtype="textual" xml:id="QuadrDed_4.lnote.oe.e" n="e" source="#liebermann"><bibl><biblScope unit="volume">1</biblScope><biblScope unit="page">529</biblScope></bibl><seg type="original" xml:lang="de"><hi rend="italic">vgl. Hn</hi> 9, 7: contingentiis</seg><seg type="translation" xml:lang="en" source="#claude"><hi rend="italic">cf. Hn</hi> 9, 7: contingentiis</seg></note>
        <note type="liebermannNote" subtype="textual" xml:id="QuadrDed_4.lnote.oe.3" n="3" source="#liebermann"><bibl><biblScope unit="volume">1</biblScope><biblScope unit="page">529</biblScope></bibl><seg type="original" xml:lang="de">crebesc. <hi rend="italic">Dm</hi></seg><seg type="translation" xml:lang="en" source="#claude">crebesc. <hi rend="italic">Dm</hi></seg></note>
        <note type="liebermannNote" subtype="textual" xml:id="QuadrDed_4.lnote.oe.a" n="a" source="#liebermann"><bibl><biblScope unit="volume">3</biblScope><biblScope unit="page">311</biblScope></bibl><seg type="original" xml:lang="de"><hi rend="italic">S. o</hi>. 2{a}</seg><seg type="translation" xml:lang="en" source="#claude"><hi rend="italic">See above</hi> 2{a}</seg></note>
        <note type="liebermannNote" subtype="textual" xml:id="QuadrDed_4.lnote.oe.b" n="b" source="#liebermann"><bibl><biblScope unit="volume">3</biblScope><biblScope unit="page">311</biblScope></bibl><seg type="original" xml:lang="de">Neuerungen beklagt Verf. öfter. <hi rend="italic">S. u</hi>. 32. Dass hier solche im Strafrecht gemeint seien, ergibt der nächste Satz über Laster.—Die folg. Abstrakta dienen dem Vf., Rechtszustände zu beschreiben: Hn 22, 1</seg><seg type="translation" xml:lang="en" source="#claude">The author often complains of innovations. <hi rend="italic">See below</hi> 32. That innovations in criminal law are meant here follows from the next sentence, on vices.—The abstract nouns that follow serve the author to describe legal conditions: Hn 22, 1</seg></note>
      </div>
    </body>
